      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so for a residential build here i'm looking at a roughly 15x20 deck. with the cedar being pretty heavy, how do i actually calculate the total load for the beam? like what's the formula or the standard psf (pounds per square foot) i should be using for the math?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For residential deck calculations, the American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCI) recommends using the 35 psf Live Load and 40 psf Dead Load, but these values can vary depending on local building codes. A more commonly used estimate is the 30 psf Live Load and 30 psf Dead Load for wood decks. To calculate the total load on the beam, multiply the live and dead loads by the area of the deck. The area of your 15x20 deck is 300 sqft, so you'd use: (30 psf x 300 sqft) for live load + (30 psf x 300 sqft) for dead load = 9,000 + 9,000 = 18,000 psf total load.",
